 A5 – Design the File Storage
A5.1 – Text or Binary?

* Donor file – Text format: Donor records are stored one per line using `fprintf()`. Text files are easy to read, edit and debug.

* Stock file – Binary format: Stock records are stored using `fwrite()`. Binary files are compact and faster to read and write.

### A5.2 – Update in Place

```text
OPEN stock file in read/write binary mode
IF file open fails
    RETURN FAILURE
END IF

SEEK to the required record
READ the stock record using fread()

IF read fails
    RETURN FAILURE
END IF

UPDATE the units

SEEK back to the same record
WRITE using fwrite()

CLOSE file
RETURN SUCCESS
```

Binary files allow direct access to records using `fseek()`, `fread()` and `fwrite()` without rewriting the entire file.

### Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

If stock is stored in a text file, the entire file must be read and rewritten to update a record.

| Method                 | Update time | File size                      |
| ---------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------ |
| Binary in-place update | O(1)        | Compact, fixed-size records    |
| Text file rewrite      | O(n)        | Usually larger, human-readable |

Binary storage is efficient for frequent stock updates, while text storage is easier for humans to read and edit.

